**EXERCISE 4.15.** (For set theorists.) Let $f : |D| \to |D|$ be a monotone function on (the elements of) a domain. Show that $f$ has a *maximal* fixed point (i.e. a fixed point that cannot be extended to a larger fixed point). (Hint: By Zorn's Lemma consider a maximal chain

$$
C \subseteq \{ x \in |D| \mid x \sqsubseteq f(x) \},
$$

and use 2.11 to remark that $\bigsqcup C \in |D|$.) Now argue that $f$ has a <u>least</u> fixed point.

Maximal and least fixed points for monotone endomaps, and the fixed-point inequality below an infimum of fixed points, are with the
[Maximal fixed points](1980_maximalFixed.md) peer.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`example_415`).** Every monotone endomap has a least fixed point and a maximal fixed point that cannot be extended upward among fixed points.

*Proof.* Invoke the least and maximal existence laws from the maximal fixed-points peer. ∎
